  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2021-10-08T23:33:27.683Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "I largely like the new QM, but have one UX issue that I find the direction on puzzling. It's nothing that's a huge problem, but I figured I'd leave some input. \nItems on the quick menu, from a UX perspective, should be the items you most frequently need to interact with and want to reach with the fewest inputs possible. The news banner is the opposite of this -- it's something I want to see when a new item is added, once, and not every time I open my menu. I don't need to see the same news tens or hundreds of times per session, and I don't want it taking up valuable real estate on a menu that could be better utilized by other features. If there's an announcement about an event or the like, that information is only useful to me the first time I see it, and a waste of space every other time. Meanwhile, functions that I absolutely want to access quickly in the heat of the moment, such as the camera, are behind an additional click, while items I'd argue are less time sensitive, like emoji, are on the launch pad. \nMy suggestion is to either have a news tab with an icon to show whether or not there are unread items, or if you're deadset on making them more visible, a wing that is only visible when there are unread items either above or below the main body of the quick menu.",
